WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.400
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.559
Today, we are living the advent of neural
network-based image generation algorithms.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:14.900
What you see here is some super high quality
results from a technique developed by scientists

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.400
at NVIDIA called StyleGAN2.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.460
Right, all of these were generated by a learning
algorithm.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.810
And, while generating images of this quality
is a great achievement, but if we have an

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:31.640
artistic vision, we wonder, can we bend these
images to our will?

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.010
Can we control them?

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:39.989
Well, kind of, and one of the methods that
enables us to do that is called image interpolation.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.800
This means that we have a reference image
for style, and a target image, and with this,

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.780
we can morph one human face into another.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.199
This is sufficient for some use cases, however,
if we are looking for more elaborate edits,

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:55.569
we hit a wall.

00:00:55.569 --> 00:01:00.089
Now it’s good that we already know what
StyleGAN is, because this new work builds

00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:05.800
on top of that, and shows exceptional image
editing and interpolation abilities.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.640
Let’s start with the image editing part!

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:18.220
With this new work, we can give anyone glasses,
and a smile, or even better, transform them

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.260
into a variant of the Mona Lisa.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.780
Beautiful.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.180
The authors of the paper call this process
semantic diffusion.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.930
Now, let’s have a closer look at the expression
and pose change possibilities.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:37.910
I really like that we have fine-grained control
over these parameters, and what’s even better,

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:44.200
we don’t just have a start and endpoint,
but all the intermediate images make sense,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.170
and can stand on their own.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:51.170
This is great for pose and expression because
we can control how big of a smile we are looking

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:57.790
for, or even better, we can adjust the age
of the test subject with remarkable granularity.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.600
Let’s go all out!

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:07.360
I like how Mr. Cumberbatch looks nearly the
same as a baby, we might have a new mathematical

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.829
definition for baby face right there, and
apparently Mr. DiCaprio scores a bit lower

00:02:12.829 --> 00:02:13.909
on that.

00:02:13.909 --> 00:02:16.800
And I would say that both results are quite
credible!

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.120
Very cool!

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.209
And now, onto image interpolation.

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:25.480
What does this new work bring to the table
in this area?

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:30.290
Previous techniques are also pretty good at
morphing…until we take a closer look at

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:31.290
them.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.900
Let’s continue our journey with three interpolation
examples, with increasing difficulty.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.530
Let’s see the easy one first.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:44.150
I was looking for morphing example with long
hair, you will see why right away.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.849
This is how the older method did.

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:48.209
Uh-oh.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:51.620
One more time.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.719
Do you see what I see?

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:58.700
If I stop the process here, you see that this
is an intermediate image that doesn’t make

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.700
sense.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.450
The hair over the forehead just suddenly vanishes
into the ether.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:09.770
Let’s see how the new method deals with
this issue!

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:17.409
Wow, much cleaner, and I can stop nearly anywhere
and leave the process with a usable image.

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:18.639
Easy example, checkmark.

00:03:18.639 --> 00:03:21.830
Now let’s see an intermediate-level example!

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:27.499
Let’s go from an old black and white Einstein
photo to a recent picture with colors and

00:03:27.499 --> 00:03:33.749
stop the process at different points, and…yes,
I prefer the picture created with the new

00:03:33.749 --> 00:03:36.999
technique close to every single time!

00:03:36.999 --> 00:03:38.090
Do you agree?

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.499
Let me know in the comments below!

00:03:40.499 --> 00:03:42.639
Intermediate example, checkmark.

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:46.450
And now, onwards to the let’s hardest, nastiest
example.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:52.139
This is going to sound impossible, but we
are going to transform the Eiffel Tower into

00:03:52.139 --> 00:03:53.689
the Tower Bridge.

00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:56.769
Yes, that sounds pretty much impossible.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:04:01.329
So let’s see how a conventional interpolation
technique did here.

00:04:01.329 --> 00:04:03.370
Well…that’s not good.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:08.269
I would argue that nearly none of the images
showcased here would be believable if we stopped

00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:10.919
the process and took them out.

00:04:10.919 --> 00:04:12.590
And let’s see the new method.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:23.330
Hmm, that makes sense, we start with one tower,
then, two towers grow from the ground, and…look!

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.319
Wow!

00:04:25.319 --> 00:04:28.120
The bridge slowly appears between them.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.120
That was incredible.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.069
While we look at some more results, what really
happened here?

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:37.169
At the risk of simplifying the contribution
of this new paper, we can say that during

00:04:37.169 --> 00:04:43.139
interpolation, it ensures that we remain within
the same domain for the intermediate images.

00:04:43.139 --> 00:04:48.639
Intuitively, as a result, we get less nonsense
in the outputs, and can pull off morphing

00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:53.780
not only between human faces, but even go
from a black and white photo to a colored

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:58.750
one, and what’s more, it can even deal with
completely different building types.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:03.160
Or, you know, just transform people into Mona
Lisa variants.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.120
Absolutely amazing.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:54.949
What a time to be alive!

00:05:54.949 --> 00:06:03.080
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.419
and I'll see you next time!

